--TEST--
`Core\Config::set('memory', …)` is accepted at the `[limits.hard]` ceiling and refused one unit above it, and the refusal leaves the ceiling the runtime enforces where it was — `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound asserted on both sides. `config-every-set-refusal-answers-false-and-moves-nothing.nvst`
// asks what a refusal answers on a host with no file at all; this one states a
// `[limits.hard]` and pins where the ceiling actually falls — 32M is the last
// value accepted and 33M the first refused, because `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set`'s comparison
// is "at or below". A member that stopped one unit early prints plausibly
// against either half alone.
echo "default=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
echo "at=", (Core\Config::set("memory", "32M") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";
echo "value=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
echo "over=", (Core\Config::set("memory", "33M") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";
echo "far=", (Core\Config::set("memory", "512M") ? "ok" : "refused"), "\n";
echo "value=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// What a `false` and an unmoved `get` still do not prove: that the *runtime*
// ignored the refused value too. So the case allocates past both — 64 MiB,
// which 512M would have permitted and 32M does not — and the breach that
// follows is the assertion. It is `rule:errors/on-limit`'s `FATAL`, so there is no `try`
// here and could not be: the goal's standing decision is that a set above the
// hard ceiling returns `false` rather than throwing, and the breach it did not
// license reaches no `catch` either.
array<string> $ballast = [];
int $slabs = 0;
while ($slabs < 64) {
    $ballast[] = Core\Str::repeat("x", 1048576);
    $slabs++;
}

// Only reachable in a tree where the refused set moved the ceiling anyway,
// which is what makes the error expectation below worth stating: without it
// this line would print and the case would pass on stdout alone.
echo "held=", Core\Arr::count($ballast), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The value in force and the ceiling over it. `[limits.hard]` is `System`, so
# nothing the program below does can reach this line — `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`.
[limits]
memory = "16M"

[limits.hard]
memory = "32M"
--EXPECT--
default=16M
at=ok
value=32M
over=refused
far=refused
value=32M
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 33554432, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler, when an allocation of %d bytes was refused
